WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Hey, welcome to Planet Terri and Daily, I'm Patrick and Vancouver, and this is Episode 33.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.900
Today we're going to move from the grasslands of South Africa to the inside of a human

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:14.820
mouth, then into glowing plants, lab-grown organs, the surprising real-world story of weight-loss

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:22.240
drugs, a cosmic collision next door, cleaner ways to turn CO2 into fuel, AI that can predict floods

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.680
with almost no local data, the tempered results coming out of psychedelic trials,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:32.560
the current state of biological AI, and finally the quiet disappearance of front gardens in

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.280
Australian suburbs. It's a wide-ranging set of findings, but each one tells us something useful

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.660
about how living systems, whether they're raptors, human cells, plants, or whole cities,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.900
respond to pressure. Let's start with the South African raptors because the numbers are sobering.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.880
Over a 16-year period from 2009 to 2025, researchers ran consistent road surveys across Central

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.180
South Africa covering the same routes again and again so they could track actual population trends

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.820
rather than snapshots. They monitored 26 species of raptors and large terrestrial birds.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.680
What they found was that half of those species experienced significant declines, and for many of them,

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.680
the drop exceeded 50%. Only three species showed clear increases over the same time frame.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.380
To put this in context, these aren't obscure birds hiding in remote wilderness.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.460
Many of these raptors are the ones you might see perched on telephone poles or gliding over

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.480
farmland as you drive through the high weld. The fact that so many of them have declined so sharply

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.700
in less than two decades suggests that the pressures, whether it's changing agricultural practices,

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.000
pesticide use, power line collisions, or broader habitat alteration, are adding up faster than

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.060
the birds can adapt. What strikes me is how quickly these shifts can happen once multiple stressors

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:52.620
align. It's a reminder that ecosystems don't always degrade gradually. Sometimes the change looks

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.760
sudden once you have long enough data to see the trend. We'll likely need more detailed follow-up to

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.060
tease apart exactly which factors matter most in which regions, but the overall message is clear.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:08.820
The top of the food chain is wobbling. Moving from large-scale ecological losses to something much

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.780
smaller and closer to home, there's a fascinating and slightly unsettling new connection between oral

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:20.820
bacteria and breast cancer. A common bacterium associated with gum disease has been shown to travel

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.300
through the bloodstream and reach breast tissue where it can cause DNA damage and accelerate both

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:32.000
tumor growth and metastasis. The effect appears to be stronger in people who carry mutations in the

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.440
BRCO-1 gene, and there are early hints that the bacterium may also reduce the effectiveness of certain

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.140
cancer therapies. Most of us were taught to think of gum disease as a strictly local problem, bleeding

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:49.440
gums, bad breath, maybe eventual tooth loss. But the reality is that your oral microbiome is constantly

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.880
interacting with your immune system, and when gums are chronically inflamed, those bacteria can slip

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.760
into the circulation. Once they reach distant tissues, they're not just passive passengers. This

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:06.900
particular species can trigger DNA damage and make tumor cells more aggressive. Your mouth harbors

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.260
hundreds of bacterial species, and even mild ongoing inflammation can create openings for them to travel.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.840
What I find particularly interesting here is how this reframes prevention. If these findings hold up

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.860
in larger studies, routine dental care and good oral hygiene could eventually become part of broader

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.520
cancer risk discussions, especially for people with known genetic vulnerabilities. Researchers are

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.380
already planning to look at whether treating periodontal disease produces measurable changes in

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:39.800
cancer progression or treatment response in at-risk groups. It's a powerful example of how two fields,

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.700
dentistry and oncology, that we usually keep in separate boxes might need to start talking to

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.920
each other more often. Staying with surprising biological connections, scientists have created a

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.640
beautiful new research tool by engineering plants that literally glow when their immune system

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.540
kicks into gear. They took bioluminescence genes from mushrooms and inserted them into the plants in

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.520
such a way that the plants light up visibly during immune responses triggered by infection stress or

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.620
insect attack. Because the glow is tied directly to the plant's own defense pathways, researchers can

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:16.480
now watch immune activation happen in real-time without having to destroy the plant or use invasive

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.120
markers. This feels elegant in a way that a lot of synthetic biology sometimes doesn't. Instead of

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.700
waiting for yellowing leaves or stunted growth to tell you something's wrong, you can see the precise

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.940
moment in location where the plant is mounting its defense. That kind of non-invasive real-time

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:37.120
monitoring could become very useful for studying crop resilience, testing new varieties or

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.760
understanding how different environmental stresses interact with plant immunity. It's still early days,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.360
but I can imagine plant breeders using these glowing lines as a quick visual screen in greenhouses

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.720
or growth chambers. It's a nice reminder that sometimes the most useful innovations are the ones

00:04:53.720 --> 00:05:00.140
that simply let us observe nature more clearly. From glowing plants to the challenge of regenerating

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:05.860
entire organs, another study this week caught my attention for very practical reasons. Researchers

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:12.680
successfully grew an autologous esophageal segment. Tissue made from the mini-pig zone cells in the lab

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.820
and then implanted it into the animal. The engineered tissue integrated well maintained its structure

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.840
and showed functional performance in this large animal model. That matters because moving from small

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:28.040
rodents to something closer to human size is one of the big translational hurdles in regenerative medicine.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:34.100
We're still years away from routine clinical use in people, but seeing a lab-grown esophagus

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:39.420
actually work in a physiologically relevant model is genuinely encouraging. It suggests the field

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:45.140
is making steady, incremental progress rather than just accumulating more proof of concept papers in

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:51.100
mice. The long-term goal is to be able to repair or replace damaged sections of esophagus after

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:56.260
cancer surgery or severe injury without having to harvest tissue from elsewhere in the patient's body.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:00.880
This study doesn't get us all the way there, but it's an important step along a very difficult

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:07.000
road. While we're still some distance from lab-grown organs becoming commonplace, real-world data on

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.240
existing treatments is already reshaping how we think about long-term health management. A large

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.320
observational study involving nearly 8,000 patients looked at what actually happens when people stop

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:24.680
taking GLP1 receptor agonists like ozempic or maujaro. The earlier clinical trials had suggested that most

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:30.420
people regain weight quickly once they discontinue the drugs. But this real-world analysis paints a

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:36.100
more optimistic picture. Many patients either maintain their weight loss or keep losing by restarting

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:40.880
treatment when needed, switching to a different agent, or making meaningful lifestyle changes.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:46.520
I think this distinction between controlled trial data and real-world experience is important.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.840
Trials are essential for establishing safety and efficacy under strict conditions,

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:55.400
but they don't always capture how people actually live with these medications over years.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:00.820
The fact that a substantial portion of patients seem able to manage their weight even after stopping

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:05.800
through some combination of continued support and behavior change could ease some of the anxiety

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.000
that surrounds these drugs. It doesn't mean the medications are a permanent cure,

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:15.080
but it does suggest the story is more nuanced than the headlines about rapid rebound would have us

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:20.480
believe. Shifting from individual metabolic health to the health of entire ecosystems and beyond

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.880
the raptor declines we opened with are part of much larger environmental changes.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:31.140
But let's travel even farther out for a moment to a galaxy not so far away. Astronomers have figured out

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:36.760
that the chaotic stellar motions inside the small Magellanic Cloud are the result of a past collision

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:42.620
with its bigger neighbor, the large Magellanic Cloud. That ancient impact also explains the

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:48.880
galaxy's unusual patterns of gas rotation. For a long time, the small Magellanic Cloud was treated as a

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.400
relatively simple typical dwarf galaxy. Now it looks like it has a more violent history than we

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.720
assumed. What we see today is the aftermath of that cosmic event millions of years ago.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:06.300
It's a useful reminder that even objects we think we understand well can turn out to have complicated

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.860
backstories once we look more carefully. The universe, it seems, is full of neighbors with bruises.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:17.100
Speaking of dramatic impacts, researchers have developed a cleaner, more efficient way to turn

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:23.280
carbon dioxide into methanol using a catalyst made of individual indium atoms rather than traditional

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.920
metal clusters. These single atom catalysts appear to convert CO2 more effectively while also lowering

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:34.740
the energy required for the reaction. An added scientific bonus is that the simpler design makes the

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.840
underlying mechanism easier to study in detail. This feels like a solid example of green chemistry

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.460
making incremental but meaningful progress. Turning CO2 into something useful, especially a liquid

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:50.600
fuel like methanol has been a longstanding goal. But efficiency and cost have always been barriers.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.920
Single atom catalysts are an elegant way to squeeze more performance out of less material while

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.060
potentially reducing unwanted side reactions. It's not going to solve the climate crisis on its own,

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.520
but it's the kind of steady advance that improves the toolkit we have for carbon utilization.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:09.700
From chemistry that helps the planet to technology that helps us predict its extremes,

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.940
there's an interesting new development in AI-driven environmental forecasting.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:19.440
Foundation models trained on vast amounts of general time series data can now predict river flows

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.800
and flood risk even in regions that have very little local hydrological monitoring. The models

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.640
don't need dense networks of river gauges or decades of high quality local records. They

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:34.560
generalize from patterns seen across many different environments. This could be particularly valuable

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.640
in parts of the world where monitoring infrastructure is limited or has been disrupted.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.160
Better flood warnings improve drought planning and more informed water resource

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.700
management become possible even in data scarce regions. It's another reminder that large AI systems

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:53.240
when thoughtfully applied can sometimes help level the playing field for places that have

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:58.680
historically been left behind by traditional scientific infrastructure. While AI is showing promise

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.700
in environmental science, its application in medicine, particularly around psychedelic compounds,

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:09.460
has faced some reality checks lately. psilocybin and related substances have been studied in clinical

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:15.420
trials for depression, PTSD, addiction, and even obesity. Despite the high hopes that built up over

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:20.660
the past decade, the results have not consistently outperformed placebo controls across these different

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:25.880
conditions. It's a useful reminder that rigorous data often moves more slowly than enthusiasm.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:31.740
The mixed findings don't mean these compounds have no value, but they do suggest we need to be more

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:37.100
precise about which patients might benefit at what doses and in combination with what kind of therapeutic

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:42.480
support. Science has a habit of tempering our expectations, and this appears to be one of those

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.220
moments. That note of caution feels appropriate as we consider the broader landscape of what some

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:54.800
people are calling biological AI, generalist systems that can tackle diverse tasks across genomics,

00:10:55.160 --> 00:11:00.900
proteomics, drug discovery, and other biological domains. A recent review lays out both the significant

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:06.740
promise and the very real current limitations of these tools. The paper is refreshingly honest about

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:12.660
where the field stands, excited but still early. We don't yet know exactly what these systems will

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.740
eventually be able to achieve, but understanding both their strengths and their blind spots now will

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:23.160
help us apply them more effectively later. It's the kind of measured stock taking that fields need as

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:30.040
they move from hype to actual utility. Finally, a story that hits close to home for anyone living in a

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:36.540
growing city. In Sydney's suburbs, the trend toward knockdown rebuilds, demolishing older houses

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:42.960
to build larger new ones is quietly removing nearly half of front gardens and more than 60% of tree

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:50.200
canopy while expanding driveways by 57%. The result is denser streetscapes with fewer trees,

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:56.300
less permeable surface, and reduced habitat for birds and insects. This isn't unique to Sydney.

00:11:56.300 --> 00:12:01.460
Similar patterns are playing out in many cities around the world as property values rise and people

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:06.940
maximize building footprints. The cumulative effect on urban heat, stormwater management, mental

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:11.500
health, and biodiversity is substantial even if no single rebuild looks dramatic on its own.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:16.660
It's a reminder that the ecological health of cities is shaped by thousands of small, seemingly

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:22.160
private decisions. Before we wrap up, it's worth keeping an eye on the developing research around

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:27.520
oral bacteria and cancer risk. Several groups are now planning studies to test whether treating

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.640
gum disease can meaningfully change therapy outcomes and at-risk patients. It's the kind of

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:37.820
unexpected connection that could quietly shift clinical practice if the data continues to line up.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:42.680
That's Planet Terri and Daily for today. If you've been enjoying the show,

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.900
a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really does help new listeners find us.

